<section class="content">

<h3>Notes for the Catechist</h3>

<h4>The story of Saint Augustine</h4>
<p>It is important that participants understand that Augustine&rsquo;s conversion was not an individual matter. He sought truth through intellectual paths, but upon finding Christ he understood that faith needs a body, a people, a community. Saint Ambrose was key in his journey. The Church is not an obstacle to faith; it is the place where faith is lived and grows.</p>

<h4>The meaning of &laquo;ekklesía&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Greek word <em>ekklesía</em> means &laquo;assembly called together.&raquo; It is not a gathering that people organize on their own; it is God who calls. The Church does not begin with us; it begins with God. That changes the perspective: we are not the ones who &laquo;make&raquo; the Church; God calls it together and we respond.</p>

<h4>The four marks of the Church</h4>
<p>It is crucial to distinguish between the Church as God wills it (one, holy, catholic, apostolic) and the Church as we see it with its human limitations. The marks are not our merits; they are gifts from Christ. The holiness of the Church does not come from our perfection, but from the presence of Christ and the Holy Spirit. It is important to address this honestly, without hiding the Church&rsquo;s wounds.</p>

<h4>The Church and scandal</h4>
<p>It is almost certain that someone will raise the scandals in the Church. Do not avoid them. Acknowledge the pain and shame with sincerity. But help distinguish: the Church is holy in her Head (Christ) and in the sacraments, even though her members are sinners. The fact that the Church continues to exist despite her sinners is, paradoxically, a sign that Christ sustains her.</p>

<h4>The baptismal priesthood</h4>
<p>Many Catholics do not know about the baptismal priesthood. Explain it clearly: we are not &laquo;passive laypeople&raquo; who just go to Mass. Every baptized person shares in the priesthood of Christ and offers their life as spiritual worship. That means your work, your studies, your family, your service to others &mdash; all of it can be an offering to God.</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Age Adaptation</h3>

<h4>🌱 Seed (ages 7-12)</h4>
<p>Use the image of the body: what would happen if a hand said &laquo;I don&rsquo;t need the body&raquo;? Play a game where each child represents a part of the body and they have to work together to accomplish something. Activities: draw the Church as a house with many rooms, color a body with different members, memorize 1 Pet 2:9 with hand signs.</p>

<h4>🌿 Sprouts (ages 13-17)</h4>
<p>Connect with their experience of belonging: do they feel part of something bigger? A team, a band, a family? The Church is like a community where each person has a unique gift. Talk about young saints (Saint John Bosco, Saint Thérèse, Blessed Carlo Acutis) as models of active belonging. The scandal in the Church is a real topic at this age; do not avoid it.</p>

<h4>🪴 Root (adults new to the faith)</h4>
<p>Many adults come with negative experiences of the Church: routine, hypocrisy, scandals. Be honest: acknowledge the wounds. But help them see the difference between the Church as a human institution and the Church as a mystery of faith. Share testimonies of vibrant communities, of saints who transformed the Church from within.</p>

<h4>🌳 Tree (formed adults)</h4>
<p>Go deeper into the ecclesiology of Vatican II: Lumen Gentium, the Church as universal sacrament of salvation, the relationship between the ministerial priesthood and the baptismal priesthood. You can quote the Church Fathers (Saint Cyprian: &laquo;He cannot have God for his Father who does not have the Church for his mother&raquo;). Address ecumenism and the relationship with other Christian confessions.</p>

</section>

