<section class="content">

<h3>Difficult Questions</h3>

<h4>&laquo;If the Church is holy, why is there so much scandal and sin in it?&raquo;</h4>
<p>This is perhaps the most painful question. The Catechism responds that the Church is &laquo;chaste and a sinner&raquo; at the same time: holy in her Head (Christ) and in the sacraments, but in need of purification in her members (CCC 823-829). The sins of Christians &mdash;including pastors&mdash; do not destroy the holiness of the Church, but they wound it gravely. Christ never abandons his Church; he purifies it continually. As Saint Augustine said: the Church is holy even though it has sinners in its midst, because Christ sanctifies it.</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why do I need the Church? Isn&rsquo;t it enough to believe in God my own way?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Christian faith is not individualistic. From the beginning, God wanted to gather a people, not save isolated individuals. Jesus did not leave a book; he left a community with structure and authority. Saint Paul says we are &laquo;members one of another&raquo; (Rom 12:5). The Church is the Body of Christ: you cannot separate the head from the body. Believing &laquo;your own way&raquo; is like wanting to have the head without the body. Moreover, the sacraments &mdash;which are the ordinary means of grace&mdash; need a community to be celebrated.</p>

<h4>&laquo;What does it mean that the Church is catholic? Isn&rsquo;t that exclusionary?&raquo;</h4>
<p>Catholic means &laquo;universal,&raquo; not &laquo;exclusionary.&raquo; The Church is catholic because Christ sent her to all peoples, not to a single group (Mk 16:15). It is the opposite of exclusionary: it is open to all cultures, languages, and peoples. Catholicity means that in every local community &mdash;your parish&mdash; the whole Church is present. It is not that there are many Churches; it is one Church present throughout the world (CCC 830-835).</p>

<h4>&laquo;Why do Catholics obey the Pope? Isn&rsquo;t that anti-democratic?&raquo;</h4>
<p>The Pope is not a political leader; he is the successor of Saint Peter, to whom Christ entrusted the mission of confirming his brothers in the faith (Lk 22:32). The Pope&rsquo;s authority is a service: to guarantee the unity of the Church and safeguard the apostolic faith. It is not anti-democratic; it is a charism of service. The Church also has structures of participation: councils, synods, parish councils. The Pope&rsquo;s authority does not eliminate the responsibility of the faithful, but sustains it (CCC 880-887).</p>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>Catechist's Prayer</h3>

<blockquote>
<p>Lord Jesus Christ,<br />
you who called the Apostles<br />
and built your Church upon Peter,<br />
give me a heart that loves her as you love her.<br />
May her wounds not scandalize me,<br />
but may her holiness spur me on.<br />
Give me wisdom to teach,<br />
patience to listen,<br />
and charity to welcome each person<br />
as a member of the same Body.<br />
Amen.</p>
</blockquote>

